WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.898
"Does Coffee Help Boost
Autophagy and Lifespan?"

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.560
When food is scarce, our body
detects this lack of nutrients

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.040
and shifts
into conservation mode,

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.680
turning up the process
of autophagy,

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:17.200
from the Greek "auto"
meaning "self"

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.040
and "phagy" meaning "to eat."

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:21.440
Autophagy means,
quite literally,

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:23.040
eating yourself.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.200
Autophagy plays
two major roles:

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:26.560
nutrient recovery

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:29.280
(breakdown products
re-entering cellular metabolism)

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:30.480
and quality control

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.320
(the removal of superfluous,
damaged,

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.400
or dangerous components).

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.880
In the modern context
of not just sufficient food,

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:38.720
but an excess of it,

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.720
our baseline rate of autophagy
is low

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:44.080
and slips down even further
as we get older.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:48.480
A decline in autophagic capacity
with age has been described

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.160
in almost all animals analyzed,
including humans.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:55.440
Autophagy appears to not only
be necessary for life extension,

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:56.640
but, also, in some cases,

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:59.200
sufficient for
increasing longevity.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:03.040
Boosting autophagy alone
can lengthen lifespan in mice

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.640
by an average of 17 percent,

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.440
as well as improve
their healthspan.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.000
No wonder autophagy
is at the forefront

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.040
of so much longevity research.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.000
The most reliable way to kick
autophagy into high gear

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.800
may be to eat less food
altogether,

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.520
but there is a downside
to caloric restriction.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:25.120
Starving yourself, as was
understated in a major review,

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.200
"generates discomfort."

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.280
There is, however,
something we can consume

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.040
that induces autophagy

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:35.760
that many people
find comforting: coffee.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.000
We've long known
that alcohol consumption

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.800
causes liver inflammation,
but an unexpected finding

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.720
was made by a group
of Norwegian researchers

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.880
back in 1986:

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:48.880
Coffee consumption was associated
with less liver inflammation.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:50.880
Subsequent studies
conducted around the world

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:52.400
replicated these results.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.760
In the United States,
for example,

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.120
those at risk for liver disease

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:57.920
who drank more than two cups
(475 ml) of coffee a day

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:00.000
appeared to have
less than half the risk

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.520
of developing
chronic liver disease

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:04.160
as those who drank less
than one cup.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:07.280
The fact that regular coffee
consumption seems protective

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.200
against the development
of fatty liver disease

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.680
gave researchers an idea.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:14.320
Since autophagy plays
such an important role

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.160
in clearing fat
out of the liver,

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:18.800
the researchers tested
to see whether caffeine

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:20.960
might have
cell-cleansing properties.

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:23.840
Indeed, it was found to be
a potent autophagy stimulant.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:26.640
So, does coffee or caffeine
extend the lifespan

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:29.120
of model organisms
like yeast and worms?

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:30.640
Yes...

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.960
and yes.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:35.840
In mice, coffee
rapidly triggered autophagy

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:39.680
within hours
at a human-equivalent dose,

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.360
and decaffeinated coffee
worked just as well.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.080
The autophagy-promoting
properties of coffee

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.120
were independent
of the caffeine content.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:50.880
Both regular and decaf

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.280
also had similar
anti-aging effects

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:56.000
on another aging pathway
in mice.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:58.560
But what about in people?

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:01.200
A systematic review
of the health impacts of coffee

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:02.640
on liver diseases concluded

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:05.360
that "daily coffee consumption
should be encouraged"

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:07.600
in patients
with chronic liver disease.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:11.040
But if coffee
enhances autophagy,

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:14.720
shouldn't its benefits extend
to a wide range of diseases?

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:17.200
Well, coffee intake
is also associated

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.200
with lower risk
of kidney disease,

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.280
along with reduced risk
of conditions

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:23.680
as varied as gout,
Parkinson's disease,

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.160
type 2 diabetes,
and skin cancer.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:30.240
Decaf was also associated with a
range of potential health benefits.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:33.200
The results are all
the more remarkable

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:36.480
given many of the studies may
have failed to adequately control

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.800
for smoking and
unhealthy food intake,

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:41.920
both of which tend to go
along with coffee drinking.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:44.160
So, coffee drinkers
appeared to be healthier

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:48.000
even despite their tendency
for less wholesome habits.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.280
Does all this translate
into living longer?

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:52.800
Apparently so.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.400
We only have
observational research

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.240
on coffee and mortality
in humans,

00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:58.960
but, to date, more than
20 studies around the world

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:02.160
following more than ten million
individuals over time

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:03.920
have found that those drinking
three cups (720 ml)

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:07.120
of coffee a day had 13 percent
lower risk of death.

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:08.960
If practiced
throughout adulthood,

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:10.320
that would be expected
to translate

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:14.400
into approximately
an extra year of life.

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:16.800
In terms of longevity,
three cups of decaf

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.160
appeared to be
just as protective

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:19.760
as three cups
of regular coffee,

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:21.520
so it's not the caffeine.

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:24.480
This is supported by data
showing the longevity link

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:29.280
regardless of whether people were
slow or fast metabolizers of caffeine.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.560
If it's not the caffeine,
then what was it?

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:36.320
Coffee contains more than
a thousand bioactive compounds.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:38.640
The polyphenol chlorogenic acid

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.840
is the most abundant antioxidant
in coffee beans,

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:45.200
so researchers started there
and found it was indeed

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:48.560
able to enhance autophagy
in cultured human cells.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:50.960
So, which brands
and types of coffee

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:54.160
have the most of this
autophagy-boosting compound?

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:58.619
We'll find out next.

